      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the most abundant and essential substance on Earth, uniquely suited to support life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, based on its physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The \"Weird\" Science of Water)\nWater is famous among scientists because it behaves very differently from almost all other liquids on Earth. \n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter: \n  * **Liquid** (oceans, rivers, rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n* **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is an exception. When water freezes into ice, its molecules form a crystal structure that takes up *more* space, making ice *less dense* than liquid water. This is why ice cubes float. If water didn't do this, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all marine life.\n* **The Universal Solvent:** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). Because of this, it acts as a carrier, transporting nutrients, chemicals, and waste through living organisms and ecosystems.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n* **Surface Tension:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion). This creates high surface tension, which is why water forms droplets and allows small insects, like water striders, to walk across its surface.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\n* **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n* **The Breakdown:** \n  * **96.5%** of Earth's water is salty ocean water.\n  * **2.5%** is freshwater,"
